// linux_arm64 `Clock` provider closure over the kernel `nanosleep` leaf
// (linux_arm64 `nanosleep` is 101). This leg does not sleep yet, for two
// reasons. Provider admission classifies an authored syscall leaf's mechanism
// but settles no native realization for it, so target lowering rejects the
// demanded leaf with `MissingBoundarySettlement`. And normalized syscall
// plans place each leaf parameter straight into a syscall register
// (`evaluate_syscall` in `calling-conventions/src/plans/call_plan_evaluation.rs`),
// so the millisecond count would reach `nanosleep` as its `timespec*` request
// pointer. The darwin leg's shape, a checked adapter over a std-private wait
// requirement that reaches `Clock`, does not carry over: linux_arm64 has no
// `poll` syscall, and `ppoll` and `nanosleep` both take a `timespec*`, while
// syscall plans admit only integer-class values.
linux_arm64 machine ClockNativeProvider::sleep(milliseconds: u32)
    satisfies Clock::sleep
    via ForeignBinding::Syscall(101);

linux_arm64 machine ClockNativeProvider::provider_defaults(defaults: &mut ClockNativeProvider) {
    defaults.select_provider<Clock, ClockNativeProvider>();
}
